 "cells": [
  {
   "metadata": {},
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "source": [
    "{\n",
    "  \"cells\": [\n",
    "    {\n",
    "      \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "      \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "      \"source\": [\n",
    "        \"# Batch FASTQ reader\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Scan the selected FASTQ folder (no subfolders), generate a length-sorted CSV and histogram for every file, and align to a matching FASTA or SnapGene `.dna` reference when available.\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Match references by the complete leading number: `1132.fastq.gz` matches `1132 LPPC EE R-S.dna`. Multiple matching references produce QC only and an ambiguity in the batch report. Missing references also produce QC only.\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"All outputs go directly into one separate output folder. Completed samples are skipped; set `OVERWRITE = True` to rebuild them. Failed or changed samples with existing outputs also require overwrite. The original input files are never modified.\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Install Python dependencies with `python -m pip install -r requirements-notebook.txt`, and install `minimap2` and `samtools` separately for alignment. Open this notebook with its working directory set to the repository folder. In the prepared cloud environment, first run `source /workspace/fastq-reader-local/activate.sh`.\\n\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"id\": \"batch-1\"\n",
    "    },\n",
    "    {\n",
    "      \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "      \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "      \"source\": [\n",
    "        \"from pathlib import Path\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"FASTQ_DIR = Path(\\\"/path/to/fastq_folder\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "        \"REFERENCE_DIR = None  # None searches FASTQ_DIR; otherwise Path(\\\"/path/to/reference_folder\\\")\\n\",\n",
    "        \"REFERENCE_FILE = None  # leave None for automatic grouping by FASTQ ID\\n\",\n",
    "        \"OUTPUT_DIR = FASTQ_DIR / \\\"results\\\"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"OVERWRITE = False\\n\",\n",
    "        \"QC_ONLY = False  # True skips reference search and alignment\\n\",\n",
    "        \"BIN_SIZE_BP = 2000\\n\",\n",
    "        \"LOG_Y = False\\n\",\n",
    "        \"THREADS = 4\\n\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "      \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "      \"id\": \"batch-2\"\n",
    "    },\n",
    "    {\n",
    "      \"cell_type\": \"code\",\n",
    "      \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "      \"source\": [\n",
    "        \"from batch_fastq_reader import run_batch\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"report = run_batch(\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    fastq_dir=FASTQ_DIR,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    reference_dir=REFERENCE_DIR,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    reference_file=REFERENCE_FILE,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    output_dir=OUTPUT_DIR,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    overwrite=OVERWRITE,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    qc_only=QC_ONLY,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    bin_size=BIN_SIZE_BP,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    log_y=LOG_Y,\\n\",\n",
    "        \"    threads=THREADS,\\n\",\n",
    "        \")\\n\",\n",
    "        \"display(report)\\n\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"execution_count\": null,\n",
    "      \"outputs\": [],\n",
    "      \"id\": \"batch-3\"\n",
    "    },\n",
    "    {\n",
    "      \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "      \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "      \"source\": [\n",
    "        \"Review the `status` and `message` columns for missing references, ambiguous matches, or failures. QC outputs are kept if alignment fails; the other samples continue processing. `batch_report.csv` contains the latest run's report.\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Each FASTQ is processed separately, even if several filenames have the same sample number. Outputs normally use the FASTQ stem (`1132_summary.csv`, `1132_length_hist.png`, `1132_sorted.bam`). When multiple FASTQs share an identical stem, their full filenames become their output prefixes to prevent collisions.\\n\"\n",
    "      ],\n",
    "      \"id\": \"batch-4\"\n",
    "    },\n",
    "    {\n",
    "      \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "      \"id\": \"igv-sessions\",\n",
    "      \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "      \"source\": [\n",
    "        \"## Open results in IGV\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Each successful alignment now generates a saved session such as `1132_igv.xml`. In IGV, choose **File \\u2192 Open Session** and select that XML file. It loads the matching reference FASTA and BAM together. Keep the XML, reference, FASTA index, BAM and BAM index in the same results folder.\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"To add sessions to existing results without rerunning the analysis, run this in a separate code cell:\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"```python\\n\",\n",
    "        \"from batch_fastq_reader import create_igv_sessions\\n\",\n",
    "        \"igv_report = create_igv_sessions(OUTPUT_DIR)\\n\",\n",
    "        \"display(igv_report)\\n\",\n",
    "        \"```\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Existing sessions are preserved. Pass `overwrite=True` only if you want to replace them.\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"### One reference, multiple FASTQs\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Automatic grouping is the default: `1183_2-3.fastq.gz`, `1183_4-6.fastq.gz`, `1183_12-17.fastq.gz`, and `1183_19-21.fastq.gz` all match one `1183` FASTA or SnapGene reference in the selected reference folder. Leave `REFERENCE_FILE = None`. They produce one combined session containing all four BAM tracks, labeled by the full FASTQ stem.\\n\",\n",
    "        \"\\n\",\n",
    "        \"Only if you want to force FASTQs with different IDs to use one reference, set `REFERENCE_FILE = Path(\\\"/path/to/plasmid.dna\\\")` (FASTA also works). This reference is used for every FASTQ, overriding ID-based folder matching. The results include individual sessions and one `<reference_name>_all_reads_igv.xml` session containing all compatible BAMs as separately labeled tracks. Open that combined XML in IGV. Combined sessions are refreshed on reruns; save customized overview sessions under a different filename.\\n\"\n",
    "      ]\n",
    "    }\n",
    "  ],\n",
